<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/foundational/money-inflation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Money is a technology with three jobs: a <strong>medium of exchange</strong> (no more
    bartering chickens for shoes), a <strong>unit of account</strong> (prices we can compare) and
    a <strong>store of value</strong> (savings that keep their meaning). Inflation is the third
    job going wrong — money quietly losing weight each year, so the same note buys less.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the slow tax — 3% inflation halves money's worth in about 23 years; drag i upward and watch it collapse" widget="plotter" config={powCfg} />

  <h2>The rule of 70</h2>
  <p>
    Divide 70 by the inflation rate and you get the approximate halving time: 70 ÷ 3 ≈ 23 years,
    70 ÷ 10 = 7 years, 70 ÷ 50 = 17 months. It works for anything compounding — population,
    interest, doubling time too.
  </p>

  <h2>Why prices rise</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Demand-pull:</strong> too much spending chasing too few goods — wartime booms, stimulus spikes.</li>
    <li><strong>Cost-push:</strong> key inputs get dearer — the oil shocks of 1973 and 1979 fed into nearly everything.</li>
    <li><strong>Money growth:</strong> print far more currency than output grows and prices must adjust. This is the hyperinflation mechanism.</li>
    <li><strong>Expectations:</strong> once people <em>expect</em> inflation, wages and prices pre-emptively climb — which is why central banks guard credibility so jealously. Most target ~2%.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When it goes wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Hyperinflation is the rule of 70 with the accelerator jammed. In Weimar Germany (1923) prices
    doubled roughly every 3–4 days; in Hungary (July 1946) about every <em>15 hours</em> — the
    fastest ever recorded; Zimbabwe (November 2008) hit prices rising ~80 billion percent in a
    month. Money stops being money; barter and foreign currency take over. The opposite trap,
    <strong>deflation</strong>, looks virtuous and isn't: falling prices reward waiting, spending
    stalls, debts grow heavier in real terms — Japan's 1990s stagnation is the textbook case.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cagan, "The Monetary Dynamics of Hyperinflation" (1956); Bank of England and Federal Reserve inflation targets; OpenStax, Principles of Economics.</p>
</TopicPage>
